This file describes the logic and structure of the software router implemented in router.c, providing a clear
and detailed explanation of each function and important code sequence.

The file defines two main global structures: arp_records, a dynamically allocated vector holding IP-to-MAC address
mappings used as an ARP table, and trie_base, which is the root of a binary prefix trie used to perform efficient 
Longest Prefix Match lookups for IP routing. The variable total_arp_records tracks the number of valid entries 
currently stored in the ARP table.

The lookup_recursive function takes a pointer to the start of the ARP table, an IP address to look up, and the number
of remaining entries. It recursively checks each entry in the table and returns the matching ARP entry if the IPs match. 
It is used by the resolve_arp function, which simplifies the call and hides the recursion logic.

The create_arp_header function dynamically allocates memory for an arp_hdr structure and fills in its fields using the 
provided sender and target IPs and MAC addresses. It sets the appropriate ARP opcode and marks the correct hardware and 
protocol types (Ethernet and IP). The build_ethernet_header function similarly allocates and fills an Ethernet header, 
specifying source and destination MAC addresses and the EtherType.

The assemble_arp_packet function combines the Ethernet and ARP headers into a complete ARP packet stored in a newly 
allocated buffer. It first creates the headers using the two previous functions, copies them into the buffer, and 
returns the pointer to the assembled packet. This is used when the router sends an ARP request for a MAC address 
it does not yet know.

The construct_icmp_packet function creates a full ICMP packet including Ethernet and IP headers. It is used both for 
responding to Echo Requests (type 8, code 0) and for sending ICMP error messages like Destination Unreachable (type 3) 
or Time Exceeded (type 11). The function fills in the Ethernet header by swapping the source and destination MAC 
addresses from the received packet. The IP header is built with standard ICMP values and a reversed source-destination 
address pair. The ICMP header is filled based on the message type and code, and the checksum is computed over the ICMP segment.

The routing trie is implemented using nodes of type trie_element, which have two branches (for bit values 0 and 1) and 
a pointer to an associated route if present. The initialize_trie_node function allocates and initializes such a node by 
setting the branches to NULL and clearing the route pointer. extract_bit retrieves a single bit from a 32-bit integer 
at a given position, which is essential for traversing the trie. The descend function accesses one of the node's branches. 
The has_associated_route function checks if a given node contains a valid routing entry.

The compute_significant_bits function calculates the number of contiguous 1s in a subnet mask, which tells us how many 
bits of the IP prefix should be used to descend into the trie. The ensure_branch_exists function checks if a desired 
branch in the trie exists and allocates it if it doesn't. The add_route_to_trie function inserts a given routing entry 
into the trie by walking through its prefix bit by bit and attaching it to the appropriate node. The determine_best_match 
function performs a longest-prefix search in the trie for a destination IP, returning the most specific matching route.

The load_routes_from_file function reads a file line by line and parses each line into a route_table_entry structure. It 
converts the IP addresses from text into binary and uses add_route_to_trie to populate the trie structure with the routing 
entries. The function returns the total number of routes loaded.

In main, the program begins by initializing the network interfaces via the init function, using the command-line arguments 
passed to the program. It creates a trie root and loads the routing table from a file using the load_routes_from_file 
function. The ARP table is allocated with a fixed size limit, and a queue is initialized to store packets that must 
wait for ARP resolution.

The main loop of the router continuously listens for incoming packets using recv_from_any_link. For each packet, it 
extracts the Ethernet header and checks the EtherType to determine whether it is an IP or ARP packet.

If it is an IP packet, the program extracts the IP header and optionally the ICMP payload. If the destination address 
matches one of the router's interfaces and the packet is an ICMP Echo Request, the router generates an Echo Reply 
using construct_icmp_packet and sends it. Otherwise, it verifies the IP header's checksum and looks up the appropriate 
route in the trie. If no route is found, an ICMP Destination Unreachable message is generated. If the TTL is too low 
(1 or less), an ICMP Time Exceeded message is sent.

If forwarding is possible, the TTL is decremented and the IP checksum is updated incrementally rather than recomputing 
from scratch. Then, the router checks if the MAC address of the next hop is known. If not, it copies the packet into 
dynamically allocated memory and places it into the queue. It also sends an ARP Request using assemble_arp_packet to 
try to resolve the next hop IP.

If the MAC address is known, the Ethernet header is updated with the source MAC of the current interface and the 
destination MAC of the next hop, and the packet is sent out through the appropriate interface.

If the incoming packet is ARP, the program checks the ARP operation field. If it is an ARP Request and the target 
IP matches one of the router's addresses, it constructs an ARP Reply and sends it. If it is an ARP Reply, the new 
IP-MAC mapping is stored in the ARP table. The program then processes the pending packet queue, checking if any 
queued packets were waiting for that MAC address. If so, it updates their Ethernet headers and forwards them.

The router uses a FIFO queue to hold packets that cannot be forwarded due to unresolved MAC addresses. Once the ARP 
Reply arrives, the queue is drained and the packets are forwarded.

The IP checksum update is optimized using an incremental formula when the TTL field is changed. The packet-building 
functions for ICMP and ARP ensure correctness by using structures that match networking standards.

This implementation accurately models the behavior of a real router, including longest-prefix IP forwarding, dynamic 
ARP resolution, ICMP error handling, and buffering of unresolved packets.

When running the router with the local checker, the implementation achieves a score of 70 points.